course: adult-life
lesson: 36
title: Big feelings in big people
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grown-ups have all the same feelings children have: anger, sadness, fear, loneliness, jealousy, embarrassment and pride. They are just better, most of the time, at keeping them in.
explain: Sometimes a feeling leaks out bigger than the problem, because other worries are piled up underneath it, and afterwards a good grown-up says sorry.
explain: Vivid example: in a house in Leeds, Dad sees muddy football boots on the new carpet and shouts so loudly the dog hides. At bedtime Mum explains he had an awful week at work, and Dad comes up and says, "Sorry, mate. The boots weren't worth all that shouting."
explain: Tears are not always sad either: Mum can cry at the end of a film because it moved her, and Grandma can cry because she misses Grandad.
explain: Knowing what a grown-up is feeling helps you be kind, and it helps you see that the feeling is usually not your fault.
fact: Anger is a big, hot feeling adults have too, and the shouting can be bigger than the problem when other worries are piled underneath.
fact: Most grown-ups calm down quickly after shouting and feel bad about it; shouting does not mean they stop loving you.
fact: If a grown-up's anger ever makes you frightened for your safety, tell another trusted adult.
fact: When a parent says "I'm sorry I shouted", they know shouting was not fair, even if you had done something wrong; you can answer "That's OK" or "Thank you".
fact: Grown-ups cry from sadness, stress, tiredness or relief; crying lets big feelings out and is normal and healthy, not a sign that everything is falling apart.
fact: A shaky voice before a hospital visit means a grown-up is scared; being brave means doing the scary thing anyway, not feeling no fear.
fact: Loneliness is missing people and company; an old person living alone after a husband or wife has died often feels it, and regular calls and visits help a lot.
fact: "Must be nice" said quietly about a friend's holiday is a small bit of jealousy; adults feel it too, and it is only a problem if it turns into unkindness.
fact: Embarrassment is the hot, awkward feeling of being seen making a mistake; laughing it off shrinks it, and other people forget it much faster than you do.
fact: Laughing kindly with someone who tripped is fine; laughing at them makes their embarrassment worse.
fact: Refusing to admit you are lost or wrong is pride; too much pride makes it hard to ask for help or say sorry.
fact: Saying "I was wrong" is a strength, not a weakness.
fact: Happy tears come when people are moved, touched or very happy; they pass quickly and leave people feeling warm.
exercise: Dad goes red in the face and shouts about muddy shoes on the carpet. What is most likely going on? | He is angry, and other worries may be making the shouting bigger | He has stopped loving you because of the shoes you left on the stairs again | He is pretending to be angry as a joke | He is ill and needs a doctor at once
exercise: Why can a grown-up's shouting be much bigger than the problem in front of them? | Other worries are piled up underneath the feeling | Grown-ups have to shout once a day to stay calm | The problem is always secretly enormous | Adults cannot hear themselves when they talk
exercise: Mum sits on the edge of your bed later and says, "I'm sorry I shouted." What does that show? | She knows shouting was not fair and is owning her mistake | She thinks you were right about everything all along and she was wrong | She wants you to apologise to her first | She is going to shout again tomorrow
exercise: What is a good thing to say back when a parent says sorry for shouting? | "That's OK" or "Thank you" | "You should be sorry, you're horrible" | Nothing at all for a week | "Only if you buy me something"
exercise: You find Mum crying quietly in the kitchen. Which is true? | Grown-ups cry too, and it is normal and not your fault | It means the family is falling apart and will soon split up for good | It means she is angry with you | Grown-ups only cry when someone has died
exercise: What can you do if you find a grown-up crying? | Ask "Are you OK?" or give a hug, without having to fix it | Leave the house so you don't see it | Tell her to stop because grown-ups are not supposed to cry in front of children | Fix the problem yourself before tea
exercise: Dad's voice shakes a little before Grandma's hospital appointment. What is he feeling? | He is scared about Grandma | He is cold from the weather | He is cross with Grandma | He is bored of hospitals
exercise: What does being brave really mean? | Doing the scary thing even though you are afraid | Never feeling afraid of anything, not even for a moment | Pretending nothing is happening | Not letting anybody see you at all
exercise: Since Grandad died, Grandma keeps saying the house is "very quiet". What does she mean? | She is lonely and misses him and the company | She has finally got some peace from the noise | Her hearing has suddenly got worse | The neighbours have all moved away
exercise: What helps a lonely grandparent most? | Regular calls, visits and small chats | One big present every few years | Leaving her alone so she can rest | Telling her to get over it quickly
exercise: Mum looks at a friend's holiday photos and says quietly, "Must be nice." What is that feeling? | A small bit of jealousy | Real anger at her friend | Pride in her own holidays | Fear of going on aeroplanes
exercise: When does jealousy become a real problem? | When it turns into being unkind to someone | As soon as you feel even the tiniest bit of it at all | Only when it is about money | Never, it is always fine to act on
exercise: Dad trips in the supermarket, goes red and laughs it off. Why does he laugh? | Laughing shrinks the embarrassment | He thinks falling over is the best fun | He wants people to laugh at him all day | He did not notice he tripped
exercise: Your uncle is clearly lost but says, "I know exactly where we are." What is stopping him admitting it? | Pride | Tiredness | Happiness | Jealousy
exercise: Why can too much pride cause trouble? | It makes it hard to ask for help or say sorry | It makes people too good at reading maps | It makes people cry at sad films and soppy songs | It makes people kinder to strangers
exercise: Mum cries at the end of a happy film. Why? | She is moved and happy, and those are happy tears | She is sad the film is over and wants her money back | She has something in her eye every time | She is upset with the actors
test: Overheard on the phone: "Yeah, I lost it with the kids over some muddy boots. Felt rotten after." How does the speaker feel about the shouting now? | Sorry and bad about it | Proud of how loud it was | Happy the kids were scared | Angry all over again at the boots
test: Your little brother says, "Mum cried in the kitchen, so it's all falling apart, innit." Put him right. | No, grown-ups cry from stress or tiredness too, and it's normal | Yes, crying always means the family is ending | Yes, and it's because he left his toys out | No, grown-ups never cry, she must be chopping onions
test: Dad mutters, "Bloody hospital letters," and his voice goes wobbly about Gran's check-up. What is under the grumpy words? | He is frightened for Gran | He hates the postman | He is bored with Gran | He thinks the letters are funny
test: Gran on the phone: "Oh, it's just me and the telly these days, love." What would cheer her up most? | Ringing and visiting her often | Sending her a bigger telly | Not bothering her so she can watch in peace | Telling her she is lucky to be alone
test: Mum, scrolling: "Ugh, Kerry's in Spain AGAIN. Alright for some." Is Mum a bad person? | No, a flash of jealousy is normal if she stays kind | Yes, anyone who ever feels jealous is a mean person deep down | Yes, she hates Kerry now | No, she is just angry about Spain
test: Big sister: "Dad fell over in Tesco and everyone looked, I wanted to die." What will most people in Tesco remember tomorrow? | Almost certainly nothing, they forget fast | Dad's face for the rest of their lives | That Dad is clumsy every day | Your sister's name and address
test: Your cousin points and howls "LOOK AT HIM, what a muppet" after Dad trips. What is the kinder way? | Laugh with Dad if he is laughing, never at him | Point and laugh even louder so that he gets the joke too | Pretend you've never seen him before | Tell everyone in the shop what happened
test: Uncle, an hour off the motorway: "Shut up, I know where we are, the satnav's wrong." What would show real strength here? | Saying "OK, I was wrong, let's check the map" | Driving even faster without a map until you find the way | Blaming the children for the noise | Turning the satnav off for good
test: Dad, after a big shout: "Sorry, kiddo, that wasn't about you. Work's been hell." What does this tell you? | Other worries made his anger bigger than the problem | You did something terrible that he will not tell you about | He enjoys shouting after work | Work wants him to shout at home
test: Mum is sobbing at the end of a wedding video and smiling at the same time. Should you worry? | No, they are happy tears and will pass | Yes, she must be very ill | Yes, she hates weddings | No, she is only pretending to cry
test: A grown-up's anger at home often makes you feel scared for your safety. What should you do? | Tell another trusted adult, like a teacher | Keep it a secret so that nobody gets cross with anyone | Shout back louder next time | Run away from home at night
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a parent's expression of anger may exceed the magnitude of the immediate provocation. | Additional, unrelated concerns may be compounding the emotion | The provocation is invariably more serious than it appears | Parental affection is withdrawn in proportion to the offence | Adults are incapable of regulating the volume of speech
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the condition of an elderly widow who states that her residence has become "very quiet". | She is experiencing loneliness following her bereavement | She is expressing satisfaction at reduced noise | She is reporting a deterioration in her hearing | She is complaining of the departure of her neighbours
exam: Whereas an adult maintains, contrary to the evidence, that he is not lost, determine the emotion most probably responsible for his assertion. | Pride | Jealousy | Loneliness | Grief
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the shedding of tears by adults? | Tears may arise from joy or being moved, as well as from sorrow | Tears in adults invariably indicate a serious family emergency or a death | Tears in adults are always a sign of anger | Adults who are in good health do not shed tears
